WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.120
One legendary TV host declined to beat&nbsp;
up Adam Sandler, so another stepped up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.360
for the right price. Here's what went&nbsp;
down behind the scenes of Happy Gilmore.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.280
Though Adam Sandler's manchild brand of&nbsp;
humor was established with his previous film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.840
"Billy Madison," "Happy Gilmore" keeps&nbsp;
the laughs while adding plenty of heart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.960
Sure, he still flies off the handle, but because&nbsp;
he's motivated by the desire to help his grandma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.960
and save her home, Happy's a more&nbsp;
likable protagonist than Billy.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.280
"We're gonna get your house&nbsp;
back. That is a promise."

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.145
"Sure."

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.240
It's no surprise that Adam Sandler would&nbsp;
grow up to make a movie about golf.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.680
His dad, Stanley Sandler,&nbsp;
was a huge fan of the sport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.720
taking Adam out on the links whenever he could.&nbsp;
Plus, Adam's love for the classic Bill Murray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.760
golf comedy "Caddyshack" is well known.&nbsp;
So a golf themed comedy was a no brainer.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.480
But it was one specific day with his dad&nbsp;
that inspired "Happy Gilmore." Out golfing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.760
with his father in New Hampshire,&nbsp;
teenage Sandler brought along his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.840
friend Kyle McDonough, who went on to&nbsp;
become a professional hockey player.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.840
"He was smacking the golf ball further&nbsp;
than everybody and my father would say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.550
'Man, this kid is incredible. He's got those hockey wrists.'"

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:20.480
Just like Happy, that brute hockey strength&nbsp;
was enough to dominate the game of golf.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.800
McDonough even beat Sandler's dad! In&nbsp;
an interview on the "Dan Patrick Show,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.080
Adam joked that his father might&nbsp;
have even liked Kyle better than him.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.280
As Happy Gilmore's nemesis, Shooter&nbsp;
McGavin, actor Christopher McDonald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.760
did such a convincing job that he's still&nbsp;
associated with the role to this day. So it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.920
might be hard to believe that he originally&nbsp;
turned down the part not just once, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.280
twice! In an interview with "And So it Begins,"&nbsp;
McDonald explained his reasons for saying no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.320
he was simply tired, and wanted to see more&nbsp;
of his family. But then, as he describes it…

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.760
"I played a round of golf in Seattle, and we won.&nbsp;
And that high was something else. So, with my golf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.960
shoes still on, I went in the locker room, called&nbsp;
my agent and said, 'Is that golf movie still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.000
available, because I just won this tournament&nbsp;
and I'm feeling a little bit…Shooterish.'"

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.880
And then, after a meeting with Adam Sandler where&nbsp;
the comedian made him laugh the entire time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.880
McDonald was convinced, and&nbsp;
Shooter McGavin was his.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.920
Before "Happy Gilmore," Bob Barker was known as&nbsp;
the mild-mannered host of "The Price is Right."

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.160
"Bet you get a lot of that&nbsp;
with "Let's Make a Deal."

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.600
"It's 'The Price is Right,' Happy."

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.680
"Oh yeah sorry."

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.440
"It happens. Let's play some golf."

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.480
This made his fist fight&nbsp;
with Happy one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.120
surprising – and hilarious – parts of the film.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.240
"You want a piece of me?"

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.400
"I don't want a piece of&nbsp;
you, I want the whole thing!"

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.920
The chemistry between Sandler and Barker&nbsp;
makes it hard to believe Barker was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.840
the first choice for the cameo. On&nbsp;
"The Dan Patrick Show," Adam Sandler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.760
revealed that when his writing buddy&nbsp;
Tim Herlihy was working on the script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.160
Ed McMahon, Johnny Carson's "Tonight&nbsp;
Show" sidekick, was his first choice.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.825
"And now, ladies and gentlemen, heeere's Johnny!"

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.400
But when they reached out to McMahon,&nbsp;
the answer was no. Sandler's theory?

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.840
"I doubt he got it. [...] I think&nbsp;
the script was a little rougher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.395
we cursed a little bit more. And then when Ed&nbsp;
said no, we dialed it down a bit for Bob Barker."

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.840
When Bob Barker was asked to make what&nbsp;
would later become the most popular cameo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.920
in "Happy Gilmore," he had already&nbsp;
spent years training with two pros:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.640
martial arts expert and actor Chuck Norris,&nbsp;
and the karate champion Pat Johnson.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.480
"Let's go! You like that old man?"

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.840
The game show host joked that his experience&nbsp;
honing his skills over those many years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.320
is why he accepted the part. In Adam&nbsp;
Sandler's biography, Barker explained,

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.680
"In twenty years I never won a fight.&nbsp;
[Norris and Johnson] both beat me up. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.160
when they offered me this script, I said,&nbsp;
'I'll do it,' because I win the fight."

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.160
Sandler recalled how, early in the morning&nbsp;
on the day of filming the showdown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.640
Barker was stretching and getting ready for&nbsp;
the fight in the bitter cold — like a boss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.280
But Barker actually had other concerns&nbsp;
— like catching pneumonia. As he puts it:

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.200
"Next time I'd like to work inside."

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.720
Now that, at the time of this video’s release,&nbsp;
Barker has reached the impressive age of 98,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.920
he may not be able to appear in a future sequel.&nbsp;
But as he's told Sandler, if it does happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:27.360
he wants more money, plus a love scene. Luckily&nbsp;
for the fans, a hilarious, epic second round&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.080
of Barker fighting Sandler did go down on&nbsp;
Comedy Central's "Night of Too Many Stars."

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.200
In the late '90s, Adam Sandler co-wrote&nbsp;
and starred in a string of hits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.680
from "The Wedding Singer" to "Big Daddy" to&nbsp;
"The Waterboy." But that success sprung from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.840
a difficult time. In 1995, after five years&nbsp;
on Saturday Night Live, Sandler was fired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.080
Recently on "The Howard Stern Show,"&nbsp;
Sandler described feeling angry and hurt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.040
and recalled commiserating with his friend,&nbsp;
comedian Chris Farley, who was also fired.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:04.160
"We got mad together, pretended we weren't&nbsp;
sad, and pretended this was for the best"

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.920
But Sandler's film career was just beginning –&nbsp;
a few months before his final "SNL" performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.560
"Billy Madison" was released to massive success.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:13.840
"Donkey Kong sucks!"

00:05:14.648 --> 00:05:16.682
"You know somethin'? You suck!"

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:25.200
And Sandler followed this up with "Happy Gilmore,"&nbsp;
which raked in an impressive 41.2 million dollars!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.400
These victories allowed Sandler, and his loyal&nbsp;
writing partner, Tim Herlihy, to establish their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:36.560
own company in 1999 — Happy Madison Productions.&nbsp;
Herlihy and Sanders have a long history together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.280
writing together from Saturday Night Live, to&nbsp;
"Happy Gilmore," to the more recent Netflix hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.280
"Murder Mystery." One of the big reasons&nbsp;
the partnership has worked so well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.280
for so long, is because they respect&nbsp;
each other's ideas and always give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.280
proper credit. In an interview&nbsp;
with Esquire, Herlihy explained,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.680
"SNL teaches you to be very generous with&nbsp;
credit [...] Adam is really good about that."

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.840
Even with all of the memorable characters in&nbsp;
"Happy Gilmore," the golf pro, Chubbs, played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:09.680
Carl Weathers, really stood out. Surprisingly,&nbsp;
Weathers was not the first choice — John Amos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:15.840
of "Good Times" fame, was. But this fact was only&nbsp;
revealed in recent years, when Amos appeared in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:21.200
"Uncut Gems" with Adam Sandler. In an interview&nbsp;
with “Vulture,” the directors of "Uncut Gems,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.760
the Safdie Brothers, revealed how Sandler&nbsp;
and Herlihy wanted Amos for Chubbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.920
and that it was the studio that decided&nbsp;
Carl Weathers would be the better choice.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:33.200
Amos later confirmed this during&nbsp;
an interview with "Insider,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.760
recalling a screen test he did for the part&nbsp;
of Chubbs. But he's over it it, saying:

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.320
"It never worked out, but no hard feelings."

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.720
Amos went on to say he's never seen "Happy&nbsp;
Gilmore," or watched other movies he didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.840
get to appear in — because to do so might stir&nbsp;
up sad feelings over roles he missed out on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.760
This is a very zen attitude that we're&nbsp;
pretty sure Kevin Nealon would approve of.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.840
"You gotta harness in the good energy and&nbsp;
block out the bad. Harness, energy, block bad."

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.160
Aside from his classic performance&nbsp;
as Chubbs in "Happy Gilmore,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.600
Carl Weathers is also well-known for his role&nbsp;
as badass Colonel Al Dillon in "Predator,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.320
alongside Arnold Schwarzenegger. In the&nbsp;
film, the duo are part of a military team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.520
sent into South America but are caught unprepared&nbsp;
when they encounter an alien. Spoilers ahead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:23.920
but Weathers' character Dillon didn't fare too&nbsp;
well in his fight with the lethal creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:28.080
a battle which resulted in the loss&nbsp;
of — you guessed it — his right arm.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.000
Fast forward to "Happy Gilmore," nearly&nbsp;
a decade later, and Weathers' Chubbs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.400
also had some terrible luck with a nasty&nbsp;
creature. This time it was an alligator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:40.480
and the animal ended up taking the hand&nbsp;
from his right arm, the same one he lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.000
in "Predator!" Was this a nod to fans of the&nbsp;
sci-fi action classic? It sure looks like it!

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.160
"Damn alligator bit my hand off!"

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:49.920
"Oh my God!"

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.000
Bob Barker loved his cameo in "Happy&nbsp;
Gilmore," but golfer Lee Trevino?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.920
Not so much! The pro athlete made a few —&nbsp;
mainly silent — appearances throughout the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.400
but when he did speak, his lines were memorable!

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.080
"Grizzly Adams did have a beard."

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.800
These brief scenes with the golf champion&nbsp;
are great examples of what gives the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.280
its wacky charm. So you might be surprised&nbsp;
to discover that Trevino didn't actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:17.280
read the script before signing on to&nbsp;
the project! If he had, he later said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.240
he wouldn't have appeared in the film. The golfer&nbsp;
made his feelings clear in an interview with CBS:

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.840
"If they were going to use all those foul&nbsp;
words in there, I never would have done it."

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:31.840
"Happy Gilmore" has many fans, including&nbsp;
pro golfers! In an interview for the "Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:36.160
Patrick Show," Christopher McDonald talked&nbsp;
about his experiences golfing with the pros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:40.240
who love trying the signature&nbsp;
Happy swing. The actor explained,

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.960
"I meet all these great players [...] They all try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.920
that shot [...] They think it's&nbsp;
the funniest thing in the world."

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.920
You might be surprised to find out that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.080
odd method is actually a legal&nbsp;
way to swing! As McDonald said,

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.160
"These guys are crushing it. But you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:57.760
that's what it takes. It takes a&nbsp;
good athlete to hit that shot."

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.560
"Just tap it in. Just tap it in. Give&nbsp;
it a little tappy. Tap tap tap-a-roo."

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.360
Fans have been clamoring for a sequel&nbsp;
to "Happy Gilmore" for quite some time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.120
And now, it looks like they&nbsp;
just might get one! In 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.640
the comedian posted a video on&nbsp;
Twitter performing the signature shot.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:20.680
"Ok, It's been 25 years since I've&nbsp;
done this, let's see what happens."

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:23.920
He even made a shout out to Shooter Mcgavin!

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.040
"I'm scared…Shooter McGavin, this is for you."

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:37.040
A few hours later, Christopher&nbsp;
McDonald responded with his own Tweet:

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:43.440
"Nice drive, Gilmore. 25 years, huh?&nbsp;
Let's see if it's Shooter's tour."

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.640
Shooting a ball into a jar in what appears&nbsp;
to be his living room, Mcdonald adds:

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:50.640
"Money. Shooter's still got it."

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.440
A few days later, in an appearance on the "Dan&nbsp;
Patrick Show," Sandler said a sequel would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:01.440
amazing. When pressed if that meant "Happy Gilmore&nbsp;
Two" was actually…happening, the comedian said:

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.760
"Yes, you can greenlight this."

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.120
And Christopher McDonald agreed,&nbsp;
adding in a later interview:

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:08.640
"It would be a complete blast!"

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.903
Sounds like a hole in one to us!

